Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'Gunsmoke Over Dust Creek', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Gold Rush', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Dust on the Wind', 'text': "the sheriff was the first to offer a meal and the news. 'the rustler king means to take it all,' the sheriff said. 'The water, the grazing, the town. Unless somebody stands up.' Elena Vargas ate slowly. Standing up was a habit.\n\nElena Vargas slept with a branding iron under the pillow and one eye open. justice rides slow but sure — out here, that's not a saying. It's the whole law.\n\nThat night in the saloon, the rustler king's men made their play: cards, insults, a gun cleared half out of leather. Elena Vargas ended it without firing — the fast peace, the sheriff called it later, shaking their head."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Claim', 'text': "the rustler king came at noon, as villains do, with lawyers and gunmen in equal number. 'a branding iron is a forgery,' the rustler king declared. 'And forgers hang.' The town watched from windows. Dust Creek held its breath again.\n\nthe sheriff stood with Elena Vargas — and then the blacksmith, and the schoolteacher, and one by one half the town. 'We know Elena Vargas's word,' the sheriff said. It wasn't much. It was everything.\n\nThursday night they came with fire. The livery burned, and a branding iron's copies with it — but Elena Vargas had the original where fire couldn't reach. the rustler king had played the last card. Friday would tell."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'High Noon', 'text': "The hearing went ahead anyway, because Elena Vargas insisted on it. Law, not guns. a branding iron was examined, the witness testified, the seal was verified. The judge — even the rustler king's judge — couldn't lie with the whole town watching.\n\nthe rustler king stepped out with two gunmen. 'Last chance,' the rustler king said. 'Ride out.' Elena Vargas thought of Dust Creek, of the sheriff, of every small place that ever needed one person to stand. 'No,' Elena Vargas said.\n\nWhat happened next took four seconds and would be told for forty years. When the dust settled, the rustler king's gunmen were down — winged, not killed; Elena Vargas never shot to kill if winging would do — and the rustler king stood alone, empty-handed."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "the rustler king wasn't done — the breed never is. The cattle were driven off in the night, the water rights challenged, the railroad brought in. Dust Creek needed more than a verdict; it needed a future.\n\nthe rustler king's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the rustler king never understood.\n\nElena Vargas organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the rustler king's middlemen. the sheriff rode drag. a branding iron — the deed — rode in Elena Vargas's breast pocket, the reason for it all."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "But Elena Vargas stayed through the winter, helping the sheriff mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of Dust Creek instead of just its defender. It suited them.\n\na branding iron stayed in the courthouse, under glass, with the iron likeness beside it. Proof. Promise. justice rides slow but sure — carved, at the sheriff's insistence, on the frame.\n\nElena Vargas was offered the sheriff's star, the mayor's office, and three ranches. Elena Vargas took none of them. 'I ride,' Elena Vargas said, which explained everything and nothing, the way true things do."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "justice rides slow but sure. In Dust Creek, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nthe sheriff keeps a room ready, and the kettle on, and the news of Dust Creek in letters. 'Come home when the trail allows,' every letter ends. The trail always allows, eventually.\n\nSpring came, and Elena Vargas rode out with the thaw, a branding iron's story told and retold in the saloon. the sheriff watched them go from the porch, and didn't wave — waving is for endings, and this wasn't one."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

