an paper.\n\nthe railroad boss's claim died that afternoon, in public, on the record. the high lonesome exhaled. the ranch foreman bought the drinks. Ada Coleman drank one, slow, tasting justice. It tasted like dust and sweet water."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "Ada Coleman organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the railroad boss's middlemen. the ranch foreman rode drag. a silver spur — the deed — rode in Ada Coleman's breast pocket, the reason for it all.\n\nthe railroad boss's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the railroad boss never understood.\n\nThe nugget tested them: stampede at midnight, Ada Coleman riding the point with a lantern and a prayer; the river crossing, the ranch foreman swimming the lead steer; the long dry stretch, sharing the last canteen."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "the high lonesome threw a dance. the ranch foreman danced every dance. The schoolteacher cried. The blacksmith forged a silver spur's likeness in iron for the courthouse wall — 'so nobody forgets,' he said.\n\nAda Coleman was offered the sheriff's star, the mayor's office, and three ranches. Ada Coleman took none of them. 'I ride,' Ada Coleman said, which explained everything and nothing, the way true things do.\n\nBut Ada Coleman stayed through the winter, helping the ranch foreman mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of the high lonesome instead of just its defender. It suited them."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "Spring came, and Ada Coleman rode out with the thaw, a silver spur's story told and retold in the saloon. the ranch foreman watched them go from the porch, and didn't wave — waving is for endings, and this wasn't one.\n\nAda Coleman rides somewhere west, where the map goes blank. There are always more valleys, more villains,